(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-pigmented-skin-lesions-quiz"]={"title":"Pigmented Skin Lesions Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What is first-line treatment for vitiligo involving more than 5% of the body surface?","choices":["Surgical tissue or cellular grafting to repopulate the depigmented skin","Systemic immunosuppression with a calcineurin inhibitor taken orally","Phototherapy, with narrow band ultraviolet B preferred over PUVA","Topical corticosteroid applied twice daily to all affected areas of skin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — PUVA carries adverse effects including increased skin cancer risk. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Describe the appearance and natural history of solar lentigines.","choices":["Well-circumscribed with regular round or oval borders, uniformly black or brown in colour, and always under 5 mm in diameter","Small light brown symmetric macules that fade in winter and regress in later life","Smooth firm black papules 1 to 5 mm across on the face and neck, identical to small keratoses","Well-defined with irregular borders, coalescing at sites of severe sunburn, from under 1 mm to several centimetres, light to dark brown"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — over time they enlarge, darken, stay stable, regress, or progress into lichenoid keratoses. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"How is naevus spilus managed?","choices":["Observation with periodic clinical evaluation, plus sun protection counselling","Excision with negative margins at the time it is first identified clinically","Cryotherapy or quality-switched laser for cosmetic removal if preferred","Magnetic resonance imaging of the brain and total spine at presentation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it rarely progresses to melanoma. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"With what other anomalies can naevus spilus be associated?","choices":["Anomalies of vascular, central nervous system or connective tissue origin","Anomalies of the endocrine system with insulin resistance and hyperandrogenism","Anomalies of the gastrointestinal tract with malabsorption and coeliac disease","Anomalies of the haematologic system with monoclonal gammopathy and myeloma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it is a variant of congenital naevus present at birth or in the first years of life. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"How are ephelides managed?","choices":["Cryotherapy as the first-line office treatment, with five to ten second freezes repeated over several sessions as needed","Surgical excision with negative margins, since the lesions carry a risk of transformation","Observation alone, since no treatment of any kind alters the appearance of the lesions","Sun protection and education first, with topical depigmenting agents; intense pulsed light or laser preferred over cryotherapy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — cryotherapy is difficult because of the size of the lesions, and laser-treated lesions can relapse. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A dark brown or black naevus on light skin is described as suspicious. What is the expected colour of a common acquired naevus?","choices":["Jet-black with shades of blue, grey or brown, sharply circumscribed","Pink or red, hairless, firm and dome-shaped on the face or trunk","Skin coloured, brown or pink, with a homogenous surface and colour","Blue, blue-grey or blue-black, with pigment sitting deep in the dermis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — they are usually under 6 mm, round to oval, and sharply demarcated. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What is the age distribution of lentigines, and how do they behave with sun exposure?","choices":["A single peak in early childhood, and they fade completely when sun exposure ceases each winter","A single peak in the thirties, after which the number of lesions declines steadily with age","Onset at birth or shortly afterwards, with the lesion growing in proportion to the child","A bimodal distribution in early childhood or later in life, and they do not fade when sun exposure stops"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — that behaviour is what separates them from ephelides. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"How is the diagnosis of Spitz naevus established?","choices":["By Wood's lamp examination in a dark room","By dermoscopy showing a moth-eaten border","By biopsy or by wide excision of the lesion","By clinical examination alone in every case"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — management is by excision, since it can resemble melanoma. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which feature defines a dysplastic naevus within the melanocytic naevus group?","choices":["Deep dermal spindle or epithelioid melanocytes","Atypical architectural and cytologic features","Origin from neural-crest derived precursor cells","Origin from junctional melanocytes in the epidermis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the other two divisions are acquired and congenital naevi by their cell of origin. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What is neurocutaneous melanosis and which patients are at risk?","choices":["A related condition in patients with multiple Spitz naevi, associated with an underlying familial cancer syndrome","A related condition in patients with naevi on the head, neck or posterior midline, causing seizures, hydrocephalus, neurological deficits and vomiting","A related condition in patients with naevi on the trunk and extremities, causing pigmented macules within a tan background patch","A related condition in patients with over one hundred naevi by adolescence, carrying a high risk of progression to melanoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the prognosis is poor once neurological symptoms appear, usually in the first few years of life. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"How are seborrheic keratoses diagnosed and managed?","choices":["Clinically, with topical fluorouracil applied for two to four weeks as field therapy","Clinically, with supportive management; cryotherapy may help if itchy or inflamed, though they recur","By biopsy in every case, with excision because they are easily mistaken for neoplasms","Clinically, with excision and negative margins required for every symptomatic lesion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — no treatment is required for asymptomatic lesions. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which topical depigmenting agents are named for ephelides?","choices":["Permethrin, ivermectin and topical metronidazole preparations","Hydroquinone, retinoids, alpha-hydroxy acids and botanicals","Tacrolimus, pimecrolimus and a mid-potency topical corticosteroid","Fluorouracil, imiquimod, tirbanibulin and diclofenac gel"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, though sun protection and counselling remain the priority. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What determines management of a congenital melanocytic naevus?","choices":["Patient age alone, with all lesions excised before the child reaches school age","Pigment intensity alone, with darker lesions excised and lighter ones observed","Melanoma risk together with cosmetic and functional considerations, removing as much as possible while preserving function","Lesion size alone, with every lesion over 5 mm excised regardless of its site or of graft availability at all"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — observation may be the better option where there is little skin for a graft site. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"How does segmental vitiligo differ from non-segmental vitiligo?","choices":["Segmental lesions are unilateral, do not cross the midline and are organised in block-like patterns","Segmental lesions are bilateral and symmetrical with a preference for periorificial facial skin","Segmental lesions fluoresce under a Wood's lamp while non-segmental lesions do not","Segmental lesions are the only form associated with other autoimmune conditions"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — non-segmental disease is well defined and symmetrical with a preference for face, genitals and acral areas. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which lesion is dermatosis papulosa nigrans described as identical to?","choices":["Small solar lentigines","Small blue naevi of the face","Small dysplastic naevi","Small seborrheic keratoses"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the distinction is in the epidemiology and the distribution on face and neck. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Describe pigmented spindle cell naevus, also called Reed naevus.","choices":["A lesion at least 5 mm with irregular indistinct borders and variable tan to brown pigmentation on sun-exposed skin","A deeply pigmented blue-black macule under 1 cm on the dorsal hands and feet, scalp, buttocks or sacral region","A sharply circumscribed darkly pigmented papule usually under 7 mm, jet-black with shades of blue, grey or brown, on the extremities and mainly the thigh","A solitary pink or red hairless firm dome-shaped papule several millimetres to centimetres across, on face, neck, trunk or extremities"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — commonest in the thirties, with females affected more than males, and it is benign. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Why is cryotherapy not used for ephelides?","choices":["The lesions are too small for it to be practical","The lesions would recur immediately after treatment","It causes post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation in them","It carries a risk of transforming them into melanoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — intense pulsed light or lasers are preferred, though lesions can relapse. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"When is surgical treatment appropriate in vitiligo, and what else is named in management?","choices":["Tissue or cellular grafting only for highly stable disease, alongside psychological intervention and cosmetic therapies","Grafting at first presentation in every patient, since earlier surgery gives a better repigmentation result","Grafting whenever phototherapy has failed after five weeks of narrow band ultraviolet B","Grafting is never used, since the depigmented areas repigment spontaneously over time"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — combining topical therapy with phototherapy is described as ideal. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"How are solar lentigines managed?","choices":["Excision with negative surgical margins in every case, because of the associated risk of skin cancer","Topical depigmenting agents only, since cryotherapy cannot be used on these lesions","Narrow band ultraviolet B phototherapy given three times weekly for five weeks","Treatment is not necessary; cosmetic removal with retinoids, cryotherapy or quality-switched laser if preferred"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the lesions are benign. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Describe the clinical appearance of ephelides and the main differential.","choices":["Beige to black papules and plaques 2 to 20 mm across, with melanoma as the main differential","White non-scaly macules with distinct margins, with tinea versicolor as the main differential","Deep blue macules under 1 cm across, with nodular melanoma as the main differential","Small light brown symmetric macules 3 to 5 mm across, with lentigines as the main differential"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — they are asymptomatic and the diagnosis is clinical. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Who is at risk of dysplastic melanocytic naevi, and what does dysplastic naevus syndrome look like?","choices":["Commonest in women in their twenties; in the syndrome the lesions are deeply pigmented and sit within the dermis","Commonest in Caucasians with a family history; in the syndrome there can be over one hundred naevi by adolescence","Commonest in African Americans and dark-skinned Asians; in the syndrome the lesions are confined to the face and neck","Commonest in patients over fifty with cumulative sun damage; in the syndrome the lesions coalesce at sunburn sites"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the higher the number of naevi, the higher the risk of melanoma. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What are the trade-offs between topical steroids and topical calcineurin inhibitors in vitiligo?","choices":["Steroids are safe for the face and for children but are expensive; calcineurin inhibitors are cheap but cause skin atrophy and raised intraocular pressure","Steroids work only above 5% body surface involvement; calcineurin inhibitors work only below it","Steroids are used only with phototherapy; calcineurin inhibitors are used only as monotherapy","Steroids are cheap with good efficacy but risk skin atrophy and raised intraocular pressure; calcineurin inhibitors are safe for face and children but carry an increased cancer risk"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — that is why calcineurin inhibitors are chosen where steroids cannot be applied. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Describe seborrheic keratoses and who gets them.","choices":["Benign deeply pigmented blue-black papules under 1 cm, common in women in their twenties","Benign beige to brown to black papules and plaques 2 to 20 mm across, velvety or warty, looking stuck onto the skin, common in older adults","Benign uniformly brown macules under 5 mm across, smooth and flat to the touch, common in early childhood or later life","Malignant scaly erythematous plaques with a sandpaper texture, common in outdoor workers"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — they are easily mistaken for neoplasms. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"When is topical therapy used in vitiligo, and what are the options?","choices":["When under 5% of the body surface is involved and in combination with phototherapy, using topical steroids or calcineurin inhibitors","When over 5% of the total body surface area is involved, using narrow band ultraviolet B alone as the sole treatment","Only after surgical grafting has failed, using hydroquinone to depigment the remaining skin","In every patient regardless of extent, using topical steroids alone without any phototherapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — tacrolimus and pimecrolimus suit face, neck, intertriginous areas and children. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"How is a blue naevus diagnosed and managed?","choices":["Clinically for small lesions and by biopsy for larger ones, with observation and biopsy or excision if changes are noted","By biopsy in all cases with immediate wide excision, since the lesions are premalignant","Clinically in all cases with cryotherapy for cosmetic removal if the patient prefers","By dermoscopy in all cases, with topical depigmenting agents applied over the course of several months"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the lesions are blue, blue-grey or blue-black. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Where do blue naevi most commonly occur?","choices":["The dorsal hands and feet, scalp, buttocks or sacral region","The trunk and extremities, with the scalp and face also affected","The lower extremities and mainly the thigh in women in their thirties","The face and neck in dark-skinned patients, with females predominating"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the lesions are blue, blue-grey or blue-black. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"How is a common acquired melanocytic naevus diagnosed and managed?","choices":["By dermoscopy in all cases, with cryotherapy applied to every lesion that has been identified","Clinically, with observation, removal for cosmetic or symptomatic reasons, and counselling on sun protection","By biopsy in all cases, with excision required because of the risk of malignant transformation","By Wood's lamp examination, with phototherapy for extensive involvement of the body surface"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — no routine excision is required. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What determines melanoma risk in a congenital melanocytic naevus, and where do they most often occur?","choices":["The darker the pigment the higher the risk, and they occur most commonly on the face and the neck","The number of lesions determines the risk, and they occur most commonly on sun-exposed surfaces","The larger the lesion the higher the risk, and they occur most commonly on the trunk and extremities","The smaller the lesion the higher the risk, and they occur most commonly on the palms and the soles"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the scalp and face are also affected, and neurofibromatosis type I is a named risk. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"How is dysplastic melanocytic naevus diagnosed and managed?","choices":["Diagnosis is clinical; management is cryotherapy or quality-switched laser for cosmetic removal","Diagnosis is by Wood's lamp; management is phototherapy for extensive body surface involvement","Diagnosis is by biopsy; management is observation, biopsy of all changing or developing lesions, excision where melanoma is a concern, and sun protection","Diagnosis is made clinically; management is observation alone with sun protection counselling, since these lesions never progress to melanoma at all"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — melanoma risk rises with the number of naevi present. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which condition is named as a risk factor associated with congenital melanocytic naevi?","choices":["LAMB and myxoma syndrome","Neurofibromatosis type I","Dysplastic naevus syndrome","Familial cancer syndrome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — congenital naevi also carry a high risk of developing into melanoma. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 28"}]};
